# Identity and alias administration. All of it needs the admin key.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5104
@adminKey = dev-admin-key-0000-not-a-secret

### 1. Register a supplier hotel code; a new canonical id is minted (201 with a Location header)
# @name mint
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/mapping/identities
X-Api-Key: {{adminKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "supplier": "supplier-a", "supplierCode": "demo-hotel-001" }

### 2. Attach another supplier's code to the same canonical hotel (201); repeating it answers 200 "alreadyExists"
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/mapping/identities
X-Api-Key: {{adminKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "supplier": "supplier-b", "supplierCode": "demo-hotel-001-b", "canonicalId": "{{mint.response.body.canonicalId}}" }

### 3. Register a second canonical hotel that will be merged away (201)
# @name second
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/mapping/identities
X-Api-Key: {{adminKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "supplier": "supplier-c", "supplierCode": "demo-hotel-002" }

### 4. Retire the second hotel in favour of the first (204). Cycles are refused with 409.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/mapping/aliases
X-Api-Key: {{adminKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "retiredId": "{{second.response.body.canonicalId}}", "survivorId": "{{mint.response.body.canonicalId}}" }

### 5. Look the retired id up: it resolves to the survivor and shows all supplier records (200)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/mapping/hotels/{{second.response.body.canonicalId}}
X-Api-Key: {{adminKey}}
